      'Raag Hamsadhwani (literally "The Cry of the Celestial Swan," where Hamsa symbolizes wisdom, purity, and the vehicle of Goddess Saraswati) is one of the most celebrated examples of cross-regional musical synthesis in India. Unlike ancient ragas whose origins are lost in antiquity, Hamsadhwani has a precisely documented birth: it was created in the late eighteenth century in Tamil Nadu by the great Carnatic composer Ramaswami Dikshitar (1735–1817 CE), father of the legendary Muthuswami Dikshitar (one of the Carnatic Trinity). Derived as an Audav-Audav Janya from the 29th Melakarta Dheerasankarabharanam (corresponding to Hindustani Bilawal Thaat), Hamsadhwani was immortally enshrined when Muthuswami Dikshitar composed the iconic Ganesha invocation "Vatapi Ganapatim Bhaje."',
      'During the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, as great Hindustani maestros such as Ustad Abdul Karim Khan (founder of the Kirana Gharana) and Ustad Aman Ali Khan (founder of the Bhendibazar Gharana) traveled across southern India and engaged in artistic dialogue with Carnatic vidwans, they fell in love with Hamsadhwani\'s sparkling, buoyant symmetry. Aman Ali Khan composed the immortal Hindustani bandish "Laagi Lagan Pati Sakhi Sang," which permanently naturalised Hamsadhwani into the North Indian concert repertoire.',
